<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Select a drug class, distinguish stockpiled supply from persistence, and calculate
proportion of days covered with an explicit follow-up denominator.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire_drugs`,
then `omop.gibleed`.

## Research question and supply patterns

For new users of a drug class, what fraction of the first year is covered by
recorded supply, and when does the first treatment era end? The questionnaire
example has three synthetic supply patterns, with 12 people per pattern:

| Pattern | Fill days from entry | Days supplied per fill |
|---|---|---|
| Continuous, with early refills | 0, 100, 220 | 120, 120, 125 |
| Two overlapping fills, then stop | 0, 30 | 60, 60 |
| Short course, then restart | 0, 120 | 30, 30 |

Stockpiling shifts overlapping supply later. For the first pattern, the raw union
of fill intervals covers 345 days, but carrying unused supply forward covers 365.
A gap over 30 days starts a new era. Predict covered days and first-era duration
before examining the summaries.

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire_drugs", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire", categories = TRUE, drug_histories = TRUE)$conns
```

## Find a class and its descendants

```{r class-discovery}
classes <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", standard_only = FALSE, datasources = conns)$pooled
classes[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "concept_class_id", "standard_concept")]
class_id <- classes$concept_id[classes$concept_class_id == "ATC 2nd"]
children <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(class_id, datasources = conns)$pooled
children[c("concept_id", "concept_name")]
ds.omop.concept.prevalence("drug_exposure", type = "both", datasources = conns)$pooled
class_set <- omop_concept_set(class_id, descendants = TRUE)
saveRDS(class_set, "utilisation-class.rds")
```

ATC classification concepts have standard status C, so the search includes
nonstandard entries. Select second-level class **960002**. Its synthetic hierarchy selects **950001**, Questionnaire
study medicine. The occurrence band 35 means 35–39 exposed people; the analysis
below has 36. Class membership comes from the hierarchy, not a drug's spelling
or a Maps to substitution.

## Define eligible time and supply

Require a 365-day washout and observed follow-up through day 364: days 0–364
contain 365 eligible days. This complete-follow-up restriction simplifies the
denominator but can select people with more stable observation. A different
study may instead use variable observed follow-up.

```{r utilisation-definition, table.digits = 3}
users <- omop_new_users(class_set, washout_days = 365)
drug <- omop_event("drug_exposure", class_set)
era <- omop_era(drug, gap = 30, by = "ingredient", stockpile = TRUE)
covered <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("covered_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364), coverage = "effective_supply"))
persistence <- omop_variable(event = era, reducer = omop_reduce("first_era_days",
  window = omop_window(0, 364)))
study <- omop_study(users, outputs = "baseline", require = list(followup = 364),
  baseline = list(covered = covered, persistence = persistence,
    discontinued = omop_number_flag(persistence, "lt", 365)))
recipe_execute(study, out = c(baseline = "D_supply"), datasources = conns)
ds.omop.mutate("D_supply", pdc = covered / 365, newobj = "D_use", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_use$pdc", datasources = conns)
```

Mean PDC is **0.4977** across 36 people: (365 + 120 + 60)/(3 × 365).
PDC is a dimensionless fraction of eligible days. It measures recorded supply,
not ingestion. This class contains one ingredient; a class with several would
also need a rule about whether switching preserves persistence.

```{r covered-days}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_use$covered",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Twelve people each have 60, 120 and 365 covered days, matching the three patterns.
The restarted course contributes 60 covered days across two eras, even though
its first era lasts only 30 days.

## Exercises

**1. Compare persistence with coverage.** Summarise first-era duration. Explain
why it differs from total covered days for the restarted course.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r persistence}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_use$persistence",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

First eras last 30, 120 and 365 days, with 12 people in each and mean **171.67**
days. The gap before the restart exceeds 30 days, so the second course does not
extend the first era. A tolerated gap can add to an era's span without adding
covered supply days.

</details>

**2. Interpret discontinuation.** How many first eras end before the first year
is complete? Does this prove permanent cessation?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r discontinuation}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_use$discontinued",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

There are **24** indicators of one and **12** of zero. The definition says the
first era ended before day 365 under this gap rule. It permits restarting, as the
third pattern demonstrates; it does not prove permanent cessation.

</details>

## Check feasibility before applying this to another resource

GiBleed has no ATC hierarchy, but [chapter 20](book-20-case-gibleed.html#find-the-drugs-and-outcome) identifies celecoxib ingredient
**1118084**. Check what its available exposure spans can support.

```{r gibleed-feasibility, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(datasources = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.gibleed", datasources = conns)
```

```{r gibleed-feasibility-local-9, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_switch("gibleed")$conns
```

```{r gibleed-feasibility-10}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("celecoxib", domain = "Drug", vocabulary = "RxNorm", datasources = conns)$pooled
hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
ingredient <- hits$concept_id[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "celecoxib"]
```

Select the standard celecoxib ingredient by its exact name. An ingredient
analysis does not become a class analysis merely by giving it an ATC label.

```{r recorded-supply, table.digits = 3}
gibleed_drug <- omop_event("drug_exposure", ingredient)
gibleed_era <- omop_era(gibleed_drug, gap = 30, by = "ingredient", stockpile = TRUE)
feasibility <- omop_study(omop_new_users(ingredient, washout_days = 365),
  outputs = "baseline", require = list(followup = 364), baseline = list(
    covered = omop_variable(event = gibleed_era, reducer = omop_reduce("covered_days",
      window = omop_window(0, 364), coverage = "effective_supply"))))
recipe_execute(feasibility, out = c(baseline = "D_feasibility"), datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_feasibility$covered", datasources = conns)
```

Every covered-days summary is one day because the source has zero days supplied
and equal exposure start/end dates. These records cannot establish longitudinal
adherence or discontinuation, so a PDC conclusion would be unsupported.

## Recap

Establish vocabulary and supply-history feasibility, then define follow-up,
stockpiling and gaps. Covered days, first-era persistence and discontinuation
answer different questions even when derived from the same prescriptions.

**Next:** [23. Case study: describing the same population across two sites](book-23-case-table1.html).
